4.3 Security Observations from Source Review
•	functions.php defines CSRF token generation and validation helpers, but some pages use a different missing includes/auth_middleware.php bootstrap. The actual protection path must be unified and tested.
•	config.php sets several session and security response-header options. Verify that this configuration is loaded by every route, including API endpoints.
•	api/checkout.php includes an M-Pesa callback route. The callback must be verified against Daraja response structure and protected against forged or repeated callbacks.
•	admin/add_product.php accepts image uploads. Verify MIME type, extension, file size, generated filename, storage location and server execution restrictions.
•	database/seed.php includes a shared demo password. Demo credentials must be changed and must never be used on a public deployment.
4.4 Security Testing Conclusion
No end-to-end security pass rate can be stated from the supplied archive alone. The source contains some protective mechanisms, but missing includes and conflicting authentication/database conventions prevent confirmation that these controls are consistently applied.
