10.2 Final Summary
The AgriMarket source archive contains a substantial set of PHP pages and supporting assets for an online produce marketplace. The intended system supports buyer and farmer accounts, product categories and listings, cart and checkout operations, orders, receipts, administration and an M-Pesa payment flow.
The static syntax review is encouraging: all 29 PHP files passed PHP 8.4.24 syntax checking. However, syntax correctness alone is not sufficient to confirm that the application is operational. The most significant findings are the missing includes/ directory, missing authentication middleware dependencies, inconsistent database models and duplicate configuration/database connection approaches.
The immediate priority is to consolidate the project into one coherent version: restore or replace the missing shared files, select one database schema, align all SQL statements and seed data with that schema, and standardise configuration and authentication. After those changes, the application should be installed on Apache/PHP with MySQL or MariaDB and the functional, security, usability, payment and regression test cases in this report should be executed.
GENERAL CONCLUSION
This review demonstrates the importance of testing both individual source files and the integration between application components. The PHP syntax checks found no parser errors, but the dependency and database review revealed issues that could prevent the website from running correctly. These findings show why successful syntax checking must be followed by installation testing, database testing, functional testing, security testing and browser-based compatibility testing.
The report deliberately distinguishes completed checks from tests that remain to be executed. Once the identified integration defects have been corrected and the full test plan has been run, the actual test results and supporting screenshots can be added to produce a final assessment report with verified pass and fail totals.
